# Copy this file to `.env.local` and paste in three real values.
# `.env.local` is gitignored. Nothing else in the app needs configuring.
#
#   cp .env.local.example .env.local
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Supabase — Project Settings -> Data API, and Project Settings -> API Keys
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ANON_KEY wants the PUBLISHABLE key (sb_publishable_...), or on an older
# project the legacy `anon` key (eyJ...). They are the same role under two
# names; the variable name here is just the long-standing convention. The value
# is passed straight to createClient(), which accepts either format.
#
# NOT the secret key (sb_secret_...) and NOT the legacy service_role key: those
# BYPASS Row Level Security. The app would still appear to work, but the
# owner_id scoping in supabase/schema.sql would stop applying and the key would
# sit in your client bundle for anyone to read.
#
# NOT the JWT keys / JWT secret either — those sign user access tokens and are
# not an API key you paste anywhere.
#
# The publishable key is safe in the browser: it grants nothing on its own.
# RLS is what actually scopes data to the signed-in user.
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=your-supabase-project-url
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-supabase-publishable-key

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Google Gemini — https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deliberately has no VITE_ prefix, so Vite will not bundle it into client code.
# It is read only by the serverless functions in `api/`.
GEMINI_API_KEY=your-gemini-api-key

# Optional. Defaults to gemini-2.5-flash, which is fast and has a generous free tier.
# GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-2.5-flash
